From 3a71fbbc7fc73f42ab20134c59afaa937c8005fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 00:28:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: use the register rows like NVIDIA's compiler, not the
 globals

The fragment compiler kept values that outlive a fetch in the "global"
registers 16-23 and made the sequencer hold at most four pixels in
flight (MAX_OUT 4, about six times slower) for every program that
touched them, because on the tablet those registers behaved like per-
lane scratch shared by the pixels in flight. That was a symptom, not a
property of the hardware: the program has to tell the hardware how many
register rows it uses, which NVIDIA's driver does
(GLOBAL_PIX_ATTR.NUM_ROWS, MAX_QID from the row count) and ours never
did.

Measured by compiling the same GLSL with NVIDIA's compiler on the tablet
(Nexus 7 userland) and replaying its streams on this driver, and with
hand-built streams (the rules are written down in docs/grate-compiler-
verification.md of the surface-rt repository):

 - overflow temporaries live in R4-R11, not 16-23; NUM_ROWS = rows - 1
   and MAX_QID = 299 >> 2, >> 3, / 12, >> 4 for 1 to 4 rows; MAX_OUT
   stays at 0x12c for every program;
 - with more than one row the program is a whole number of rounds of
   `rows` steps. Interpolation, SFU and fetch steps sit on a multiple of
   `rows` (the unit works on row step % rows), the output write is on
   the first step of the last round, every round has an ALU packet on
   its first step, and a step that is followed by ALU, MFU or TEX work
   in the same round ends with a packet that writes nothing (two
   dependent ALU packets need one in between, a round change gives it
   for free);
 - the output stage no longer goes through four scratch registers: the
   slots of an ALU packet read their sources before any slot writes, so
   the colour channels are written straight into R2/R3. Plain textured
   drawing stays a single-row program;
 - the SFU is done as NVIDIA does it: one op per step on a row register
   R0-R3 (R4 and up do not work as its operand), the result replaces the
   register and the ALU of the same step reads it; EX2 is PREEX2 then
   EX2, POW is LG2, a multiply by the exponent, PREEX2, EX2. The
   allocator keeps values that live across an SFU op out of the rows;
 - the blend stage appended by create_blend_blob() becomes the first
   step of one more round, followed by empty steps.

Verified against a CPU model of the eight pixels-in-flight test shaders
(worst error 1/255 at 200x150 and 1024x768), bit for bit against
NVIDIA's compiled programs replayed on the driver (s4, s6), and by
running 21 self-checking tests with the previous and the new driver:
identical output. Shaders 0, 1, 2 and 5 of the pixels-in-flight test,
which the compiler used to refuse, now compile and are correct. Full-
screen fill at 1024x768: texture 92 -> 134 Mpixel/s, texture with a
coordinate transform 68 -> 92, a 4-register ALU chain 3.4 -> 29, dual-
source text blending 1.8 -> 16.

Not done yet: the link record of NVIDIA's container is not parsed (our
own lane map is used and only a single vec2 varying was compared),
programs that need more registers than R4-R15 are still refused (no
spill buffer), and the earlier fragment compiler commits still describe
the old behaviour in their messages.
